WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
From an eval to a test suite.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:05.528
An eval you run by hand when you remember to is a measurement.

3
00:00:05.808 --> 00:00:18.288
An eval that runs automatically and can block a merge is a regression test, and it changes how a team works: nobody has to remember, and nobody can quietly ship a prompt that breaks last month’s fixes.

4
00:00:18.568 --> 00:00:23.688
Anthropic’s guide to agent evals draws a useful line between two kinds of suite.

5
00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:30.368
Capability evals ask what the system can do and should start with a low pass rate, giving you a hill to climb.

6
00:00:30.648 --> 00:00:36.088
Regression evals ask whether it still does what it used to and should sit near 100 percent.

7
00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:41.296
As capability tasks become reliably solved, they graduate into the regression suite.

8
00:00:41.576 --> 00:00:50.472
The two kinds want different handling: a capability score is something you report with an interval and discuss; a regression suite is something you gate on.

9
00:00:50.752 --> 00:00:52.587
What goes into the regression suite?

10
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:59.075
The failure modes you found in error analysis (week 1), each turned into a few concrete cases.

11
00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:03.216
Every production bug that reached a user, as a case that would have caught it.

12
00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:10.301
Must-pass cases: safety refusals, the account-deletion flow, anything where one failure is unacceptable.

13
00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:15.872
And a broad sample of ordinary traffic, so the suite reflects what users actually ask.

14
00:01:16.152 --> 00:01:30.445
Hamel Husain and Shreya Shankar describe this repeatable eval set growing over time, often to 100 or more examples, with coverage of important workflows and known failures deciding its size, not a target number.

15
00:01:30.725 --> 00:01:33.008
This is where engineering habits pay off.

16
00:01:33.288 --> 00:01:41.501
The suite is code: versioned, reviewed, and run by CI (continuous integration, the system that runs your tests on every change).

17
00:01:41.781 --> 00:01:45.216
Its graders are code too, and they need their own unit tests.
